Play haptics: a platform hook, a public `Window::perform_haptic`, and the long-press haptic from the gesture tuning.

What: `gpui::Haptic`, Flutter's `HapticFeedback` calls (vibrate, light / medium / heavy impact, selection click,
success / warning / error notification). `PlatformWindow::perform_haptic(Haptic)`, a no-op by default (desktops:
Flutter plays none there), and `Window::perform_haptic(kind)` for apps. `GestureTuning` gains `long_press_haptic:
Option<Haptic>` (default `None`); when an element claims a long press (prevents default on its started event, as
GPUI's long-press tooltip does), the window plays it once. Backends, each ported from Flutter 3.50.1's embedder:
- gpui_ios: `FlutterPlatformPlugin.mm`'s `vibrateHapticFeedback:`: `UIImpactFeedbackGenerator` light / medium /
  heavy, `UISelectionFeedbackGenerator`, `UINotificationFeedbackGenerator`, and AudioToolbox's
  `AudioServicesPlaySystemSound(kSystemSoundID_Vibrate)` for vibrate (adds objc2-ui-kit's four feedback-generator
  features and links AudioToolbox).
- gpui_web: `platform_dispatcher.dart`'s `_getHapticFeedbackDuration` (10 to 50 ms per kind) through
  `navigator.vibrate`, only where the browser has it (Safari has not), as `safe_browser_api.dart`'s `vibrate`.
- The test platform records each haptic (`TestWindow::haptics`). A window test has one row per case: claimed with
  vibrate, claimed with heavy impact, unclaimed (nothing plays), claimed with no haptic (nothing plays).

Why: Parallax's platform behaviour table ports Flutter's per-platform `Feedback.forLongPress` (Android vibrate, iOS
heavy impact, desktops none) and Flutter's embedders play each `HapticFeedback` kind natively, but GPUI had no way to
play one: no platform method, no window API, and nothing that fires on a long press. The table reaches GPUI only
through `App::set_gesture_tuning` (patch 0014), so the long-press haptic rides on `GestureTuning` like the slops and
physics: one source, no second channel. Flutter's widgets call `Feedback.forLongPress` when they handle a long press;
GPUI's equivalent of handling is claiming, so an unclaimed long press (a slow tap) plays nothing. Android has no
upstream backend; parallax-android implements the method with Flutter's `PlatformPlugin.vibrateHapticFeedback`.
No cfg in core: the hook is the same on every platform and each backend decides what it plays.

Upstream PR: not filed yet.

